{%- comment -%}
  Metro Timeline — all canvas geometry is computed client-side.

  transform.js is a pure data normalizer (events with start_min/end_min,
  owner/co_owners, side, track styling; weather milestones; now_min). The
  <script> below does everything that depends on the real rendered size:

    1. Orientation — "auto" runs the timeline left-to-right on a landscape
       canvas and top-to-bottom on a portrait one (or the setting forces one).
    2. Time window — if the canvas can't give every hour enough pixels, the
       visible window narrows around "now" and events outside it are
       summarised as "+N earlier / +N later" at the ends of the line.
    3. Spine — every track's line runs the full length of the canvas in
       one bundle. The hour axis runs down the MIDDLE of the bundle (work
       lines on one side of it, family lines on the other), so no branch
       ever has to cross the hour labels.
    4. Stations + branches — each event is a ring on its owner's line at
       its true start time. A branch leaves at 45°, runs along a "lane"
       parallel to the spine carrying the label, and — if the event is
       long enough — comes back at 45° to rejoin the line at its end time.
       Short events end in a stub instead.
    5. Lane placement — labels are measured (real DOM boxes), then each
       event takes the innermost lane where neither its label nor its
       line collides with what's already there. Same-owner branches that
       overlap in a lane become ONE spur with several rings feeding into
       it (the label just slides along it); a title that would overrun
       the end of the axis loses its time tag, then gets an ellipsis, and
       only then branches backward; when nothing fits the event is counted
       as "+N more". Text steps down a size ladder (drop location → smaller
       title → drop time) only as far as needed to keep every event.
    6. Spine position — the bundle is placed so the side that needs more
       lanes gets more room, instead of always sitting dead-centre.

  Labels are plain text on the canvas (no cards, pills, or borders).
  Colours are mixed from the theme's own ink and paper (see solver/draw.js),
  so they follow the device and dark mode; every line also has its own dash
  pattern so it stays identifiable on a 1-bit panel.
{%- endcomment -%}

{% template main %}

<div class="layout layout--col gap--none metro-root">

  {%- comment -%}
    THE STRIP IS THE HEADER.
    There was a band above the map here: a logo, the word "Today", a date
    badge and a forecast, 58px deep on every panel -- an eighth of an
    800x480 board and a fourteenth of an X, taken off the map on every
    render. Four of the five things it did, the hour strip was already
    doing: what time it is now, which day each half of a two-day board
    belongs to, what has scrolled off either end, and where the scale
    changes rate. The fifth was the weather, and the weather is a fact
    about a DAY, so it belongs at that day's own end of the scale rather
    than in a row above all of them. `dayBadge` draws all of it now.
    The board already knew how to do without this band: every slot too
    shallow to spare it has been dropping it for as long as there have been
    slots, and the strip has been naming the day there ever since.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="divider divider--on-white flex-none"></div>

  <div class="metro-canvas relative grow w--full" data-metro-view="{{ view }}">
    <svg class="metro-svg absolute"></svg>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Two things the board cannot say for itself, and the one part of the old
    header that had nowhere else to go. A line missing because its feed has
    been unreachable for hours looks exactly like a line whose owner has
    nothing on today, and a forecast replayed from the last good fetch looks
    exactly like a fresh one. Under the map rather than over it: they are
    rare, they are prose, and the map is what the reader came for.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if data.calendars_down and data.calendars_down.size > 0 %}
    {%- assign down_names = data.calendars_down | join: ", " -%}
    {%- assign feed_down_tpl = data.i18n.feed_down | default: "{n} unavailable" -%}
    {%- assign feed_down_msg = feed_down_tpl | replace: "{n}", down_names -%}
    <div class="metro-alerts label label--small text--muted 1bit:text--default px--3 flex-none">{{ feed_down_msg }}</div>
  {% endif %}
  {% if data.weather_stale %}
    <div class="metro-alerts label label--small text--muted 1bit:text--default px--3 flex-none">{{ data.i18n.weather_stale | default: "Forecast may be out of date" }}</div>
  {% endif %}
  {%- comment -%}
    The service banner. A sibling of the canvas rather than a band reserved
    inside it: the canvas is the flex child that grows, so a footer here
    takes its height off the canvas and the script, which measures the
    canvas's own box, adapts with no arithmetic of its own and no second
    place for the two to disagree. When nothing is breached transform.js
    sends null, this renders nothing at all, and the map has the height
    back.
    Solid ink, paper text, the way the hour strip is: `inverse` is the same
    class the strip's panel wears, so the banner is the header's colours
    at the other end of the board rather than a colour of its own, and the
    weather icon beside it is an adaptive one, a mask painted in the band's
    paper. Armed in the markup rather than left to the framework's pass over
    the page, the way the strip's own icons are, so it is white however
    early the page is read. "Weather" is a label in
    the framework's error colour, which the framework maps to whatever
    each panel can show, and the message is set larger than a label because
    it is the one sentence on the board. Wrapping is allowed because the
    translations are not the same length and a German banner that would
    not fit on one line should take two rather than be cut.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if data.service_alert %}
    <div class="metro-banner title inverse bg--canvas rounded--small flex flex--row flex--left flex--center-y gap--small flex-none w--full px--3 py--1"
         data-metro-alert="{{ data.service_alert.kind }}">
      {%- if data.service_alert.icon -%}
        <img class="metro-banner-icon image--adaptive flex-none" src="{{ data.service_alert.icon }}" alt=""
             data-adaptive="true" style="--framework-icon-src: url('{{ data.service_alert.icon }}')">
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if data.service_alert.label -%}
        <span class="metro-banner-label label label--error 1bit:label--filled 2bit:label--filled 4bit:label--filled text--bold flex-none">{{ data.service_alert.label }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      <span class="metro-banner-text">{{ data.service_alert.text }}</span>
    </div>
  {% endif %}

<style>
  /* The banner's colours, spacing and rounding come from the framework's
     own classes on the element, because the inline-style lint counts a
     handful of property names by scanning the raw markup for them, comments
     included, against a budget of six for the whole template. Naming one
     here costs the same as using it. What is left is the icon's size and
     letting a long message wrap inside the row rather than push past it. */
  .metro-banner-text { min-width: 0; }
  .metro-banner-icon { width: 1.5em; height: 1.5em; }
  .metro-canvas { overflow: hidden; }
  /* THE BOARD'S OWN INK, AND ONLY THE INK.
     Text SIZE is not here: the board's words wear the framework's own label
     classes, which size themselves from the screen's scale, so an X panel
     gets bigger text than an OG without this file knowing the difference --
     and the ruler in measure-dom.js measures the same class it will be
     drawn in, which is what makes the measurement and the drawing agree.
     What is left is stroke and fill, which the framework has no class for. */
  .metro-svg text { fill: #000; }
  .metro-svg .rail, .metro-svg .spur { fill: none; stroke: #000; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  .metro-svg .bar, .metro-svg .tick { stroke: #000; stroke-linecap: round; }
  .metro-svg .dot { fill: #fff; stroke: #000; }
  .metro-svg .pill { fill: none; stroke: #000; stroke-width: 3; }
  .metro-svg .badge { fill: #fff; stroke: #000; stroke-width: 1.5; }
  /* the drawing surface fills its container; kept here rather than inline so
     the markup carries no style attribute of its own */
  .metro-svg { overflow: visible; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; pointer-events: none; }
  /* No box behind a label — an opaque panel over a metro map reads as a
     hole punched in it, and on a busy board those holes are most of the
     picture. The words wear the framework's `text-stroke` instead, put on
     them where they are built (solver/measure-dom.js, solver/draw.js): an
     outline in the colour of the ground, legible where a line runs under it
     and invisible where nothing does. It is drawn outside the glyphs, so the
     letterforms keep their real weight, and it follows `inverse` and dark
     mode because it is the framework's colour and not ours. */
  /* A CAPTION IS ONE THING, NOT A STACK OF ROWS.
     The time and the title are two lines of the same name, so they are set
     as tight as two lines can be set: at 1.2 the gap between them read as
     the gap between two captions, which on a board where a dozen names lean
     past each other is exactly the wrong thing for it to read as. Tighter
     also makes every label thinner, and a label's thickness is what the
     lane ladder is built out of. */
  .metro-label { line-height: 1.05; }
  .metro-hour, .metro-axis-note { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* A SECOND DAY COSTS THE ROW WHAT THE CONDITION WAS SPENDING.
     The header is one row and its height comes off the map, so a two-day
     header that wraps has taken a band of canvas to say something the
     board was already saying. The condition text is the first thing to go,
     the same way it goes when the header is squeezed: "Rain - 80% rain"
     twice over is most of the width and the numbers beside it carry the
     day anyway. Kept where there is genuinely room, which is the large
     header on a panel like the X. */
  /* A row of days is a legend for the axis below it, so it starts where the
     axis starts rather than being centred over the panel. The header is a
     column that centres what it holds, and a row shrink-wrapped to its own
     words cannot line up with anything: it takes the full width first, and
     then its contents are free to sit where the axis puts them. */
  /* A DAY'S NAME OVER ITS OWN STRETCH OF AXIS, as far as that can be had.
     Their WIDTHS cannot be the day's width -- an evening is a third of the
     axis and "Thu 10 Sep 21/13 Rain - 80% rain" does not fit a third of an
     800px panel at any size worth reading -- and their STARTS cannot be
     the day's start either: measured on a real board, aligning them meant
     deleting today's forecast to make room, which is trading information
     for tidiness the wrong way round.
     What is left is worth having on its own. The row spans the map rather
     than floating centred over it, and the days sit along it in the order
     the map draws them, first at the leading edge and last at the trailing
     one. The date marker on the strip still says exactly where the day
     turns over; this says which days are on the board and what the weather
     does on each. */
  /* The spacer between two days carries `grow`, so with nothing else said
     it takes all the slack and the last day sits at the trailing edge --
     which is where it belongs when the alignment below cannot reach. Where
     it can, the client gives that spacer an exact width instead, and
     space-between would have gone on redistributing the slack around it. */
  /* ...and the header itself, which is shrink-wrapped and centred over the
     panel like everything else in that column. A band 639px wide over a
     780px map cannot be a legend for it whatever its contents do. */
  /* Which day of a week-long holiday this is goes the way the condition
     and the alert lines go when the header is squeezed: it is an
     explanation, and the NAME of the day is what has to survive. */
  /* the alert lines are the first thing to go when the header is squeezed:
     they are an explanation, and a squeezed header has no room to explain */
  /* A tiny view (a quadrant, a half-horizontal on an OG panel) already
     hides the date and the weather for want of room, which leaves a band of
     paper carrying the mark and the word "Today" and nothing else. On a
     quadrant that band is a third of the board. The hour scale underneath
     says which day this is by saying what time it is, so the header goes
     and the map gets the height. */
  .metro-wicon { width: 28px; height: 28px; }
  .metro-mark { width: 30px; height: 30px; }
  /* Concentric rings, sized to sit on the date's own line rather than to
     be a mark in its own right: this one is punctuation in front of a
     name, not a station on a map. */
  .metro-holiday-ring { width: 14px; height: 14px; }
  /* A NAME TOO LONG FOR THE BADGE IS DROPPED, NOT CUT.
     This was a third of the row at most, with the overflow clipped: the
     holiday sat in a header band the width of the board, where a third of it
     was generous and cutting a long name was better than pushing the weather
     onto a second line and taking 85px of canvas with it.
     The badge is not a row. It is shrink-wrapped and absolutely placed, so a
     percentage resolves against something much smaller and "Boxing Day" came
     out as "Boxing Da". It does not need the clamp either: the badge is
     measured before it is drawn and gives way in a stated order when it will
     not fit its stretch of axis (see dayBadge), so a name too long to fit
     costs the badge its ordinal, then its forecast, then the name -- each of
     them whole. Nothing on the strip is ever half a word.
     Neither wraps: a badge is one row, and the row's depth is the map's. */
  .metro-holiday { min-width: 0; }
  .metro-holiday-name, .metro-holiday-day { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* A solid ink badge with the text knocked out of it. On e-ink this is the
     highest-contrast mark available, and it gives the header two fixed
     anchors — what day it is, and how much of it is on screen — that read at
     a glance from across a room instead of as grey annotations. */
  .metro-pill {
    display: inline-block; white-space: nowrap;
    padding: 2px 10px; border-radius: 999px;
    /* one declaration for both pills: the linter counts property NAMES in
       the markup, and every extra one is spend against a small budget */
    background: var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
    color: var(--framework-semantic-canvas-bg-color, #fff);
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .metro-pill--quiet {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px var(--framework-semantic-text-primary-text-color, #000);
  }
  .metro-terminus { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* A date marker on the hour strip is set in capitals, so that a name
     among numbers reads as a different KIND of thing at a glance rather
     than as another tick with letters in it. It is the same size as the
     hours: bigger, it would be the loudest thing on a strip whose job is
     the clock. */
  .metro-daybreak { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  .metro-sky { white-space: nowrap; }
  .metro-hour, .metro-axis-note, .metro-terminus, .metro-sky { padding: 1px 3px; }
  /* nothing above or below: see the line-height note. Either side is the
     paper the words sit on and stays. */
  .metro-label { padding: var(--metro-inset-across, 0) var(--metro-inset-along, 3px); }
  /* The quiet grey words are the framework's `text--muted`, which is a grey
     on paper and a light grey in `inverse`, so a shed count and the low
     temperature read on the black panel as well as on the map. The badge is its own
     background and is built without an outline: white text with a white
     outline behind it is a white blob. */
  .metro-axis-note.metro-pill { padding: 1px 9px; }
</style>

<script>
(function () {
  // ON `window`, AND THEN ALIASED, so the minifier cannot rename it. A
  // property name is not an identifier: `window.METRO` survives the mangler
  // untouched, which is what lets the layout suite swap a fixture into the
  // artefact that actually ships and measure THAT rather than the source.
  window.METRO = {{ data | json }};
  var METRO = window.METRO;
  var SCRIPT = document.currentScript;
  var ROOT = SCRIPT && SCRIPT.parentElement ? SCRIPT.parentElement : document;
  var canvas = ROOT.querySelector('.metro-canvas');
  var svg = ROOT.querySelector('.metro-svg');
  if (!canvas || !svg) return;

/* ---- solver bundle: generated from solver/ by plugin/bundle.js ---- */
// Do not edit here. Edit solver/*.js and run `node plugin/bundle.js`.
var SOLVER = (function () {
  var MODS = {};
  function require(n) { return MODS[n.replace(/^\.\//, "")]; }
  MODS["board"] = (function () {
    var module = { exports: {} }; var exports = module.exports;

// A BOARD IS DATA, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS FILE.
//
// The board the plugin draws today does not exist anywhere as a value. It is
// smeared across DOM boxes, `_`-prefixed fields hung on event objects, and a
// drawing pass that recomputes geometry a fifth time on its way out. Three
// consequences, and all three are why this has been fix-one-break-three:
//
//   1. NOTHING CAN BE CHECKED WITHOUT A BROWSER. Asking "do two captions
//      overlap" means building the plugin, launching Chromium, rendering and
//      reading the DOM back. Four minutes a run. At that price you change
//      three things before you measure, and then you cannot say which one
//      did what.
//   2. A PART OF THE BOARD CAN BE MISSING AND NOTHING SAYS SO. Five captions
//      are currently measured as zero width and placed as if they were
//      points, while drawing a hundred pixels wide. A caption with no size
//      collides with nothing, so every pass downstream is reasoning about a
//      board that is not the one on the panel.
//   3. THE SOLVER CANNOT SEE MOST OF IT. The band solver is handed counts,
//      not boxes, so it sizes the paper without knowing what goes on it.
//
// So: one structure that holds everything the panel shows, in pixels, with
// nothing implied and nothing derived at read time. It is produced by the
// solver, printed by `ascii`, checked by `check`, and only then drawn. If it
// cannot be printed as text it is not finished, and if `check` is clean the
// board is correct by construction rather than by a suite agreeing.
//
// COORDINATES. One convention, everywhere, and it is not x/y: `a` runs ALONG
// the time axis and `c` runs ACROSS it. On a landscape board a is x and c is
// y; standing up they swap. Every quantity in here is pixels in (a, c), and
// the one place that knows about x and y is the renderer. Mixing the two up
// is the single most common bug in the code this replaces, so the names do
// not permit it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the model

// A caption. `w` and `h` are the MEASURED size of the words: a caption may
// not exist without them, which is the bug in (2) above made unrepresentable.
function Caption(spec) {
  if (!(spec.w > 0) || !(spec.h > 0)) {
    throw new Error('caption "' + spec.text + '" has no size (w=' + spec.w
      + ', h=' + spec.h + '); measure before placing');
  }
  this.id = spec.id;
  this.of = spec.of || null;        // the event it names
  // THE MINUTE IT IS ABOUT. Not used by the solver -- a caption is placed by
  // where its words fit, and the mark is already in `stops` -- but the drawing
  // needs it to run a leader from the mark to the words, and working it back
  // out from the stop list is exactly the kind of rederivation this model
  // exists to stop.
  this.at = spec.at == null ? null : spec.at;
  this.text = spec.text || '';
  this.a = spec.a;                  // near edge along the axis
  this.c = spec.c;                  // near edge across it
  this.w = spec.w;                  // extent along the axis
  this.h = spec.h;                  // extent across it
  this.line = spec.line || null;    // the track it belongs to, for pairing
  this.pill = spec.pill || null;    // or the bar or box it hangs off
  this.rows = spec.rows || [this.text];  // the lines of words, as drawn
  this.size = spec.size || 1;
  // The framework class each row is drawn in, so the drawing wears exactly
  // what the ruler measured.
  this.rowCls = spec.rowCls || null;
  this.el = spec.el || null;
}
Caption.prototype.box = function () {
  return { a0: this.a, a1: this.a + this.w, c0: this.c, c1: this.c + this.h };
};

// A track's rail, as the polyline it is actually drawn as. Not a baseline
// plus a list of exceptions: the points are the drawing, so anything that
// asks where a line is at a minute gets the answer the panel shows.
function Line(spec) {
  this.key = spec.key;
  this.pts = spec.pts || [];        // [[a, c], ...] in axis order
  this.width = spec.width || 2;
  // Set when this is a SPUR rather than a trunk: the owner's key. Dropped on
  // the floor once, and it mattered -- every branch was counted as a trunk,
  // so each collected the reward meant for a trunk changing level and none
  // ever paid the price of being a second rail. The search reached for a
  // branch where a step would have done, which is exactly what it was being
  // told not to do.
  this.branchOf = spec.branchOf || null;
  // Drawn in another line's ink: a tie's spur leaves the outermost line in
  // the tie but belongs to the event's owner.
  this.ink = spec.ink || null;
  // solid | dashed | dotted -- the only thing that tells two rails apart on
  // a panel with no colour.
  this.style = spec.style || 'solid';
}
// THE BOX THIS LINE LIVES IN, worked out once.
//
// Most questions asked of a line are "are you anywhere near this caption",
// and for most lines on most boards the answer is no. Answering that by
// sampling the polyline fifty times is the single most expensive thing the
// solver does: a band search prices a thousand allocations, each of which
// asks every caption about every line, and almost all of it is spent proving
// that a rail two bands away is two bands away.
Line.prototype.bounds = function () {
  if (this._b) return this._b;
  var p = this.pts, lo = Infinity, hi = -Infinity;
  for (var i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {
    if (p[i][1] < lo) lo = p[i][1];
    if (p[i][1] > hi) hi = p[i][1];
  }
  this._b = { a0: p.length ? p[0][0] : 0, a1: p.length ? p[p.length - 1][0] : 0,
              c0: lo, c1: hi };
  return this._b;
};
// Could this line possibly be inside that box? Cheap, and only ever says
// "no" when it is certain.
//
// SEGMENT BY SEGMENT, OVER THE STRETCH THE BOX OCCUPIES. The whole line's
// extent is the cheap test and it is the only one this used to do -- which
// was fine while every rail stayed in its own band. Once a rail dives across
// the board to an interchange and back, its overall extent is most of the
// panel and a filter built on it stops filtering: every caption then samples
// every rail, which is most of what the solver spends its time on.
//
// The global box is still asked FIRST, because it is memoised and answers no
// for most pairs in a few instructions. What follows only runs for the ones
// it cannot dismiss, and it exits at the first segment that really is in the
// box rather than measuring the rest.
Line.prototype.near = function (box) {
  var p = this.pts, n = p.length;
  if (n < 2) return false;
  if (box.a1 < p[0][0] || box.a0 > p[n - 1][0]) return false;
  var b = this.bounds();
  if (b.c1 <= box.c0 || b.c0 >= box.c1) return false;
  for (var i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    var x0 = p[i - 1][0], x1 = p[i][0];
    if (x1 < box.a0 || x0 > box.a1) continue;
    var c0 = p[i - 1][1], c1 = p[i][1], d0, d1;
    if (x1 > x0) {
      // the segment clipped to the box's own stretch of the axis
      var t0 = box.a0 > x0 ? (box.a0 - x0) / (x1 - x0) : 0;
      var t1 = box.a1 < x1 ? (box.a1 - x0) / (x1 - x0) : 1;
      d0 = c0 + (c1 - c0) * t0; d1 = c0 + (c1 - c0) * t1;
    } else { d0 = c0; d1 = c1; }
    var lo = d0 < d1 ? d0 : d1, hi = d0 < d1 ? d1 : d0;
    if (hi > box.c0 && lo < box.c1) return true;
  }
  return false;
};

// Where this line is at minute-position `a`, or null WHERE IT IS NOT.
//
// This used to hold its first and last value forever in both directions,
// which is harmless for a trunk -- a trunk spans the whole board by
// construction -- and wrong for anything that stops. A branch that halts at
// its event's tick went on being reported at that level for the rest of the
// day, so the picture drew it to the right-hand edge and every test that
// asks "is a line in the way" answered yes for a rail that is not there.
//
// A line is somewhere only between its own ends.
Line.prototype.cAt = function (a) {
  var p = this.pts;
  if (!p.length) return null;
  if (a < p[0][0] - 0.001 || a > p[p.length - 1][0] + 0.001) return null;
  if (a <= p[0][0]) return p[0][1];
  if (a >= p[p.length - 1][0]) return p[p.length - 1][1];
  for (var i = 1; i < p.length; i++) {
    if (a > p[i][0]) continue;
    var a0 = p[i - 1][0], c0 = p[i - 1][1], a1 = p[i][0], c1 = p[i][1];
    if (a1 === a0) return c1;
    return c0 + (c1 - c0) * (a - a0) / (a1 - a0);
  }
  return p[p.length - 1][1];
};

// A stop: where an event starts or ends on its line.
function Stop(spec) {
  this.line = spec.line;
  this.a = spec.a;
  this.c = spec.c;
  this.kind = spec.kind || 'start';  // 'start' (a dot) or 'end' (a tick)
  this.todo = !!spec.todo;           // a task's start is a square, not a dot
}

// A CONVERGENCE: one event that several lines are at.
//
// On a transit map this is an interchange, and it is drawn as one heavy bar
// across the lines that meet there rather than as a stop on each of them.
// That is not decoration: dinner with four people is ONE dinner, and drawn as
// four separate stops with four copies of the name it reads as four things
// happening at once.
//
// So it has one name, and the name belongs to the bar rather than to any of
// the lines -- which is why a convergence's caption is the awkward one in
